/**
 * @page evaluation Evaluation: scoring one candidate
 *
 * Evaluation answers how good one @c Individual is. It defines objectives,
 * scaling, metric policy, and failure handling; it does not choose candidates,
 * own Taskflow scheduling, or perform coefficient fitting. Sampled execution
 * and static bounds are supplied by @ref execution. Optional shape enforcement
 * is documented in @ref shape_constraints.
 *
 * @section evaluation-contract Evaluate then Score
 *
 * @c EvaluatorBase::operator()(rng, individual, scratch) is @c final. It calls
 * @c Evaluate first, then calls @c Score only when evaluation does not report an
 * interpreter error. The two-argument facade owns a training-range-sized scratch
 * buffer and forwards to this composed entrypoint.
 *
 * @dot
 * digraph evaluator_phases {
 *   rankdir=TB;
 *   graph [bgcolor="transparent", pad=0.25, nodesep=0.45, ranksep=0.7];
 *   node [fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10];
 *   edge [fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=9, color="#4a5568", fontcolor="#4a5568", arrowsize=0.7];
 *   start [shape=circle, label="", width=0.18];
 *   evaluate [shape=box, style="rounded", label="Evaluate\n(sampled execution result)"];
 *   outcome [shape=diamond, label="Interpreter error?"];
 *   proof [shape=box, style="rounded", label="Optional EvaluatedBuffer"];
 *   score [shape=box, style="rounded", label="Score\n(objectives -> fitness)"];
 *   error [shape=box, style="rounded", label="ErrMax"];
 *   finish [shape=doublecircle, label="", width=0.18];
 *   start -> evaluate -> outcome;
 *   outcome -> error [label="yes"];
 *   outcome -> proof [label="no"];
 *   proof -> score -> finish;
 *   error -> finish;
 * }
 * @enddot
 *
 * @c Evaluate returns an interpreter error, an empty optional, or an
 * @c EvaluatedBuffer. The buffer is a move-only proof that the supplied scratch
 * prefix holds values for this exact individual. Value-based scorers must use
 * @c Values(individual, scratch), which checks both identities before exposing
 * the values. @c ScoreContext is call-local and must not be retained.
 *
 * @section evaluation-scoring Objective and failure policy
 *
 * @c Score converts the evaluation result into a fitness vector. Concrete
 * evaluators apply linear scaling and an error metric here; information criteria
 * and custom objective policies belong here as well. An interpreter error
 * produces @c ErrMax. A shape-policy rejection produces its configured worst
 * fitness through the decorator score path, not an @c InterpreterError.
 *
 * @c Problem::LinearScalingOmitsNonFinite and evaluator non-finite handling
 * must agree. Otherwise fitting and scoring can use different effective samples.
 * Linear scaling is distinct from coefficient fitting: the latter is a
 * search-side operation described in @ref search_optimization.
 *
 * Counter meaning is phase-specific. Concrete evaluators count forward work in
 * @c ResidualEvaluations; each @c Score override increments @c CallCount once.
 * The base entrypoint increments @c CallCount for an @c Evaluate error before
 * returning @c ErrMax.
 *
 * @section evaluation-entrypoints API entry points
 *
 * - @c EvaluatorBase, @c EvaluatedBuffer, and @c ScoreContext in
 *   @c include/operon/operators/evaluator.hpp.
 * - Concrete metric evaluators in @c source/operators/evaluator.cpp.
 * - @ref shape_constraints for hard rejection, penalties, and feasibility.
 */
